family: M0
corpus: C0
modes: bare-user-shared
tier: T0
outcome: full
severity: interop
identity: full
oracle: checksum-agreement
evidence: ostrya::commit (cross-mode commit identity across archive, bare-user,
 and bare-user-shared)
spec: format-reference.md#port-extension-bare-user-shared-mode
note: The tool cannot read this mode, so the oracle is the cross-mode identity
 invariant rather than a direct comparison. Object bytes match the bare-user
 form; only the inode mode differs.

family: M0
corpus: C1
modes: archive bare bare-user bare-user-only bare-user-shared
tier: T0
outcome: unobserved
severity: interop
identity: unobserved
oracle: checksum-agreement manifest
evidence: -
question: Confirm every mode bit in the corpus round-trips. All five values lie
 within 0775, so bare-user-only should lose nothing; confirm that it does not.

family: M0
corpus: C2
modes: archive bare bare-user bare-user-shared
tier: T0
outcome: unobserved
severity: interop
identity: unobserved
oracle: checksum-agreement manifest
evidence: -
spec: format-reference.md#repository-modes-and-on-disk-storage
question: Confirm setuid, setgid, and the sticky bit survive a commit and a
 checkout in each mode. In bare-user the logical mode lives in
 user.ostreemeta while the inode mode is forced, so the bit should survive
 logically; confirm it does.

family: M0
corpus: C2
modes: bare-user-only
tier: T0
outcome: unobserved
severity: interop
identity: unobserved
oracle: exit-status checksum-agreement
evidence: -
spec: format-reference.md#repository-modes-and-on-disk-storage
question: This mode limits regular-file bits to 0775, so the setuid, setgid, and
 sticky bits have nowhere to live. Observe whether the tool refuses the commit,
 drops the bits silently, or stores them, and whether directories behave the same
 as regular files. The tool offers a --bareuseronly-files option on pull that
 rejects files with bits outside 0775, which suggests rejection is at least one
 available behavior.

family: M0
corpus: C3
modes: archive bare-user bare-user-shared
tier: T0
outcome: unobserved
severity: interop
identity: unobserved
oracle: checksum-agreement
evidence: tests/fixtures/generated/MANIFEST (the archive and bare-user fixtures
 are generated with owner 0:0)
question: The fixture generator already commits owner 0:0 into archive and
 bare-user unprivileged, so this should hold. Confirm it, and extend to
 bare-user-shared.

family: M0
corpus: C3
modes: bare-user-only
tier: T0
outcome: lossy
severity: interop
identity: not-required
oracle: manifest
loss: The uid and gid are discarded and read back as 0. The mode stores no
 user.ostreemeta.
evidence: -
spec: format-reference.md#repository-modes-and-on-disk-storage
question: Confirm the read-back value is 0 for both ids, and confirm the commit
 checksum still records the declared ids so that a pull into a
 checksum-preserving mode recovers them.

family: M0
corpus: C3
modes: bare
tier: T3
outcome: needs-priv
severity: interop
identity: unobserved
oracle: checksum-agreement exit-status
evidence: -
spec: format-reference.md#repository-modes-and-on-disk-storage
question: Record what each implementation does when a non-root writer commits
 declared ids it does not own. The ids go on the object inode, so the chown
 fails. Capture both refusal texts and exit statuses, then confirm agreement at
 real root.

